# Python Project Rules

## Testing
- ALWAYS run tests after generating or modifying code
- Use pytest for all tests: `poetry run pytest`
- Run tests with coverage: `poetry run pytest --cov`
- Minimum 80% coverage for new code
- Tests must pass before committing
- Use fixtures for shared test data
- Parametrize tests for multiple scenarios

## Package Management
- Use poetry ONLY (never pip, pipenv, or conda directly)
- Always commit poetry.lock file
- Run `poetry install` after pyproject.toml changes
- Use `poetry add` to add dependencies
- Use `poetry add --group dev` for dev dependencies
- Check for security issues: `poetry audit` (requires plugin)

## Code Style
- Run Ruff for linting: `poetry run ruff check`
- Run Black for formatting: `poetry run black .`
- Run isort for import sorting: `poetry run isort .`
- Run mypy for type checking: `poetry run mypy src`
- All quality checks must pass before committing

## Python Best Practices
- Use type hints for all function parameters and return values
- Use `from collections.abc` for abstract types (Sequence, Mapping, etc.)
- Prefer `pathlib.Path` over string paths
- Use dataclasses or Pydantic models for data structures
- Use context managers (`with` statement) for resources
- Prefer f-strings for string formatting
- Use `Final` for constants

## Code Organization
- Source code in `src/package_name/`
- Tests in `tests/` (mirror src structure)
- One class per file (generally)
- Group related functions in modules
- Use `__init__.py` for public API exports
- Include `py.typed` file for PEP 561 compliance

## Type Hints
- Always use type hints (strict mypy enabled)
- Use `Optional[T]` for potentially None values
- Use `Sequence[T]` instead of `List[T]` for parameters
- Use `Mapping[K, V]` instead of `Dict[K, V]` for parameters
- Use `Protocol` for structural subtyping
- Use `TypeVar` for generic functions

## Testing Guidelines
- Use descriptive test function names (test_what_when_then)
- One assertion per test (generally)
- Use pytest fixtures for setup
- Use pytest.parametrize for multiple test cases
- Mock external dependencies
- Test both happy path and error cases

## Error Handling
- Use specific exception types
- Include error context in exception messages
- Log errors with structured information
- Use Result types for expected failures
- Document exceptions in docstrings

## Documentation
- Use Google-style docstrings for all public functions/classes
- Include type information in docstrings
- Provide usage examples in docstrings
- Update README.md for user-facing changes
- Document complex algorithms inline

## Security
- Never commit secrets or API keys
- Use environment variables for configuration
- Validate all user inputs
- Sanitize data before logging
- Use parameterized queries for databases
- Keep dependencies up to date

## Performance
- Use generators for large sequences
- Profile before optimizing
- Cache expensive computations
- Use appropriate data structures
- Avoid premature optimization

## Async Code
- Use async/await for I/O-bound operations
- Use asyncio.gather for parallel operations
- Set timeouts for external calls
- Handle cancellation properly
- Use structured concurrency (taskgroups)

## Git Commits
- Follow conventional commits format
- Reference issue numbers in commit body
- Keep commits focused and atomic
- Write clear commit messages

## Project-Specific Conventions
- Entry point: `src/package_name/main.py`
- Package root: `src/package_name/`
- Tests: `tests/`
- Use `poetry run` prefix for all commands

## Quality Checklist Before PR
- [ ] All tests pass with coverage ≥80%
- [ ] Ruff linting passes
- [ ] Black formatting applied
- [ ] isort import sorting applied
- [ ] mypy type checking passes
- [ ] Documentation updated
- [ ] No print() statements (use logging)
- [ ] No TODOs without linked issues
- [ ] Type hints added for all functions